  "summary": "I wrote src/main.ts with a custom GLSL water material on a 251×251 vertex grid (320 units wide), so the waves are real geometry. The vertex shader displaces the grid with five summed directional sine waves and computes normals analytically. The fragment shader adds an fbm-noise ripple normal (faded with distance), a Schlick fresnel that blends deep-water colour into a reflected sky gradient, a sun specular glint, and distance haze toward the horizon colour. The sky is `sky.gradient` with a sun placed to match the shader's sun direction. The camera sits at y=4.5 looking toward the horizon, and the `clk` uniform is updated every frame via `setUniform`. Colours are output in linear space and left to the engine's tone mapping. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after `app.ready()`. Typecheck passes. I couldn't run the app or `vite build` here, so I haven't seen the rendered result.",
